  "title": "How to Work with Quadratic Graphs",
  "content": "<p>A <strong>quadratic graph</strong> is a U-shaped (or ∩-shaped) curve called a <strong>parabola</strong>, given by \\(y = ax^2 + bx + c\\).</p><p>If \\(a > 0\\) the parabola opens upward (minimum); if \\(a < 0\\) it opens downward (maximum).</p><p>The <strong>roots</strong> (or solutions) are where the curve crosses the x-axis (\\(y = 0\\)). The <strong>turning point</strong> is the vertex.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Sketch \\(y = x^2 - 4x + 3\\).</strong></p><p>Roots: \\(x^2 - 4x + 3 = (x-1)(x-3) = 0\\) → \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = 3\\).</p><p>Turning point: \\(x = \\frac{1+3}{2} = 2\\), \\(y = 4 - 8 + 3 = -1\\). Vertex at \\((2, -1)\\).</p>"
 },
